<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Praisos</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 145 BCE (approx.) · capital Praisos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">East Cretan city of the Eteocretans, a pre-Greek people whose language survives in a few undeciphered inscriptions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Praisos stood on hills in the interior of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> say its people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eteocretans">Eteocretans</a>, 'true Cretans' descended from the island's pre-Greek inhabitants, and the settlement continued from the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Inscriptions from Praisos, written in the Greek alphabet between the 6th and 3rd centuries BCE, record a non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> that remains undeciphered. The city controlled the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diktaean-zeus">Diktaean Zeus</a> for a time and quarrelled with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/itanos">Itanos</a>. It had harbours on both coasts of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Praisos was destroyed by its neighbour <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hierapytna">Hierapytna</a> c. 145 BCE, which took over its land. Its inscriptions are the main evidence for the Eteocretan language.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/praisos.jpg" alt="Praisos" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Olaf Tausch · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Praisos_1._Akropolis_25.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 145 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Praisos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cretan religion; cult of Diktaean Zeus</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Eteocretan; Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Praesus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-422&amp;focus=praisos" aria-label="Praisos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5341 907 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Praisos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5661 1163L5652 1167"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5652 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5661 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5661 1163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-422&amp;focus=praisos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

